Takauji ASHIKAGA was a busho (Japanese military commander) from the late Kamakura period to the period of the Northern and Southern Courts (Japan).
He was the first Seii Taishogun (literally, "great general who subdues the barbarians") of the Muromachi bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun).
His real family name was Genji (Minamoto clan).
He was born as the legitimate son of Sadachika ASHIKAGA, who was a distinguished family of Kawachi-Genji (Minamoto clan).
At first, he was given a portion of the imina (personal name) of Takatoki HOJO, and called himself Takauji.
When Emperor Godaigo raised an army at Mt. <unk> in Hoki Province in Genko, he led the army of bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun) as a powerful gokenin (an immediate vassal of the shogunate) in the Kamakura bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun), but he raised an army in <unk> Shrine in Tanba Province and destroyed Rokuhara Tandai (an administrative and judicial agency in Rokuhara, Kyoto).
He was regarded as the first achievement of the fall of the bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun), and he was given one character of his imina (personal name) of Emperor Godaigo's imina (personal name), and changed his name to Takauji.
While Godaigo's Kenmu Restoration lost its support and suppressed the Nakasendai War, he stayed in Kamakura and established his own political power in Kamakura.
Due to this, the relationship with the Imperial Court became worse, and when he went to Kyoto, he forced Godaigo to go to Mt. Hiei for a while, but he once escaped to Kyushu due to a counterattack by Godaigo.
After returning to Kyoto from Kyushu, he was appointed to seii taishogun (literally, "great general who subdues the barbarians") by the retired Emperor Kogon and Emperor Komyo, and established a new samurai government (the Muromachi bakufu [Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun]).
Godaigo moved to Yoshino and founded the Southern Court (Japan).
After he founded the bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun), he established two politics with his younger brother Tadayoshi ASHIKAGA, but later opposed to Tadayoshi and developed into the Kanno Disturbance.
Although the war ended due to the death of Tadayoshi, he continued to suppress the <unk> forces such as the Southern Court and made efforts to stabilize the rule.
After Godaigo died (died), Tenryu-ji Temple was built to pray for his soul.
Since he rose in revolt against Emperor Godaigo (Emperor Godaigo), he was regarded as a rebel after the Meiji period, but after the Second World War, his personality changed greatly due to the change of historical view.
